KZ3 patch 1095 (SPU pass 70): host threads that run SPU code get the SPU's single-precision denormal rule
(MXCSR FTZ|DAZ). SPU ISA v1.2 s.9.1 p.195: denorms are not supported and are treated as zero; an operation that
would generate a denorm generates zero, a denorm operand is treated as zero. The helpers run the host's SSE ops
under the thread's MXCSR, which stayed at the IEEE default, so results differed from the SPU (a denormal where the
SPU gives 0) and every op with a denormal operand or result took a microcode assist (26.5 vs 1.5 ns per dependent
mul+add pair on an i7-12700KF; a decaying tail y *= 0.99 settles on a denormal fixed point under IEEE rounding and
runs ~20x slower than under FTZ, host test). spu_fpmode.h (new,
header-only) holds the rule; spu_drain.c exports spu_host_fp_init(), called at the start of the SPURS kernel, lane,
PM worker and job-chain threads (cellSpurs.c), SPU thread groups/tasks (spu_async_run) and raw SPUs.
Opt-in: KZ3_SPU_FTZ=1 (the K2 pair of pass 70, spu-70-k2ftz vs spu-70-k2old, showed no change in the audio
jobs' times or the holes: the hot mixer loops see normal values in the game, so denormals are not why the audio
SPU falls behind; the semantic change waits for its own A/B before a default flip). Residual deviations: x86 FTZ keeps the sign of an underflowed result (the SPU
gives +0); FTZ/DAZ also act on double precision (the ISA allows forcing DP denormal operands to zero, s.9.2, but
keeps DP denormal results). Host test tools/spu/test_spu_ftz.sh. Upstream candidate.

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): `spu_host_fp_init` in upstream's `raw_spu_run`.

